Explaination - Exceptions, Assertions, Error handling Exceptions: It is an event that occurs during the execution of a program that disrupts the program's normal flow. Python executes the except block when the conditions in the try block are not satisfied. Ex: "try: ..... except : ...." Assertions: Assertions are debugging tools that tests for conditions in a code during the execution of a program, if the condition is not satisfied, it raises an AssertionError exception. Assertions are used to confirm that a particular condition is met or true. If this condition is not met, it raises AssertionError. Ex: assert condition, "Message to print on raising AssertionError" Error handling : Error handling is a mechanism to handle errors and exceptions in your code, so that your program doesn't crash when something goes wrong. Example: try, except, finally. The try block contains some code which might potentially cause an exception. If that exception occurs, the "except" block will execute. Similarly, you can also use "else" and "finally" clauses along with "try" to achieve more control over your code. We can use the "try, except, else, finally" syntax.
