#assertions are checked during the interactive Python run only. 
#You could also use assertions in Python for debugging purposes, 
#making sure certain conditions hold true. 
#If not, the interpreter raises an AssertionError.

#assertions can be a powerful tool for detecting programmer errors quickly. 
#Otherwise, they can serve as a "sanity check" to ensure certain conditions are met before proceeding.

#Assertions in Python can be disabled using the -O command-line option or the pytest.Config.option.assertions value
#can be set in PyTest configuration file
#This will disable all assertions regardless of their state. 
#Otherwise, when assertions are disabled.
#You can add -OO option to the python command to disable assert statements and __debug__ also.


#NOTE : The assertions are also used in Python for debugging.
#You also use assertions in your tests to ensure your tests are correct.
#When the code is in production, you remove the assertions and let it run as usual but a more professional alternative could be to use try/except blocks.


#Asserts that can be run are useful to help debug code.
#If an assertion fails, Python raises an AssertionError with an optional error message. 
#When the python interpreter is run with -O or -OO option, assertions are disabled, 
#and the code will not behave as asserts describe.
#If the assert is disabled through the -OO option, then the code will behave as if asserts were removed.
#Assertion check is present during development but removed in production. 
#It is a good practice to handle this with try/except blocks for your production code.

#Example of use with try/except
#try:
#    assert something, 'error message'
#except AssertionError as e:
#    print(e)  # will print 'error message'
#
#In case of an error, the except block will be executed, and the program will continue to run.
#In case of a successful assertion, the program will print ('error message') and go ahead its way.
#It's good practice to handle these possible anomalies like errors, exceptions better in your production environment
#with specific try/except blocks to handle the situation.


#Assertions also serve as a useful way to make our code clearer, 
#because they clearly mark our intention: 
#"I understand that this should be true, or else I'll get an AssertionError"

#Assertions are useful for debugging tests, 
#making sure that the tests are set up correctly, 
#or just for being on the lookout for issues when you know the code you are testing is correct.

#Assertions in production code are most useful when used with try and except statements, 
#so that the program continues to function, while providing notification of a problem. 
#This might mean that the error has been silently corrected, 
#the program continues to run, 
#or even logging the error and trying to correct it without causing the application to crash.

#Assertions in production code is not meant to be used as a way of replacing proper validation/data integrity checking, 
#which should be enforced at all stages at both the business and data level, 
#and used as a guard for any logic that can go wrong.

#If assertions are used for these other purposes, then they would be considered an error because 
#assertions are not used for checking the program's internal consistencies and data integrity, 
#but rather for checking the state of your code itself during the--------------------    assert amnt < frm.money and amnt > 0  

